{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1190161-old-west-civil-war-firearms-for-chronicles-of-darkness","id":1190161,"title":"Old West/Civil War Firearms for Chronicles of Darkness","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-02-27T23:41:25-08:00","id":1190161,"title":"Old West/Civil War Firearms for Chronicles of Darkness","content":"Hello everyone,<br /> <br /> This is kind of an odd first post, but I registered here with the express purpose of sharing some content that I've been working on for my current game, a CoD/Hunter game set in New Mexico in 1876. I love the combination of horror and Western themes, and I set out on this campaign with fond memories of the old Savage West Werewolf game, but wanting to do something more in line with the Chronicles of Darkness &quot;feel&quot; and new crunch. I set out, merrily, to find what other players had created in terms of nineteenth-century content and rules, and discovered... nothing. I'm sure other people have tried this before, but if they have, I couldn't find it. The campaign itself is running and running well, but I've had to do a tremendous amount of work on making the content (particularly items) &quot;setting-friendly.&quot; I share it here (starting with this content) in the hopes that someone else wanting to do the same might find it useful.<br /> <br /> What follows is my adaptation of about seventy-five years' worth of firearm designs into the CoD system. I based the original stats on the only examples that exist in the nWoD rules, i.e. the Winchester Repeater and the Colt Single Action Army (better known as the Peacemaker), and then extrapolated, deduced, researched, and invented, converting to CoD by reducing all damages by one from the Armory equivalent. Wherever possible, details are real-wrold accurate; several days were spent poring over various databases of firearm history. All of the manufacturers and models are real. Liberty was taken only to fill in a gap I couldn't fill by research. You may note that a number of special properties have been invented to simulate various quirks of nineteenth-century firearms; these are only suggestions, but my own playtesting has proved they work well to differentiate otherwise-similar models.<br /> <br /> Although it is not standard in CoD, I allow my players to take the Fighting Styles: Combat Marksmanship Merit up to the third dot (but not up to five; multiple attacks are way too OP) to simulate burst autofire with a semi-automatic. For a single-action revolver, this represents the Western art of &quot;fanning,&quot; keeping the trigger depressed and manually rapid-firing the revolver by running one's hand rapidly over the hammer and allowing it to snap back, and therefore requires a free hand; for a double-action revolver, it may be done with a revolver in both hands and therefore extend the burst to medium. This is the <i>only</i> benefit to having two guns besides not having to reload, which, given the long reloading times on a cap-and-ball revolver, is very useful if combat lasts longer than five or six turns (although, to be fair, in WoD this is not a very common occurrence).<br /> <br /> One final note: I have included base prices in 1876 dollars, as I chose to use money instead of straight Resources dots in my campaign to suggest the &quot;living from dollar to dollar&quot; feel of old outlaw Westerns. It would be simple enough to convert these; I'd say up to $50 is one dot, $100 two, and $150 three.<br /> <br /> With all those caveats, I welcome your thoughts and suggestions, and hope this is of use to some or any of you. God knows I've spent way too much time creating it.<br /> _<br /> _ <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table\" style=\"border-spacing: 0px; width: 1025px\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_tr\"><